  "notes": [
   "FUJIFILM 200 Color Negative Film (current product; the bulletin compares its processing and printing with Fujicolor C200), Product Information Bulletin, plots on page 5 (nos. 15, 16 and 17); the drawings are vector paths, traced end to end. Issue date 09 FEB 2022 as printed. Process printed on the plots: CN-16 (the bulletin also allows CN-16Q/FA/L/S and Process C-41). Sheet id fuji-200, PDF fuji-fujifilm-200.pdf.",
   "Speed: daylight ISO 200/24 degrees; 3200 K tungsten ISO 50/18 degrees with Wratten 80A (Fuji LBB-12). No Log H Ref and no RMS granularity value is printed in this bulletin (log_h_ref and grain null). The bulletin says processing and printing are almost the same as for C200.",
   "curves: x is log H in lux-seconds as printed (daylight, 1/125 s); all three curves are solid and never touch (D-min plateaus about 0.82 b, 0.37 g, 0.13 r; the curves end at log H about 0.27 b, 0.37 g, 0.33 r). The last point of a line can lie up to one 0.05 step beyond the drawn end (flat extrapolation by the tool).",
   "sensitivity: the y axis has NO numbers, only a double arrow marking 1.0 between two horizontal rules. y is therefore set to 1.0 on the upper rule and 0.0 on the lower rule: the zero is arbitrary, only differences between points and between layers are meaningful. Sensitivity = reciprocal of the exposure in J/cm2 giving density 1.0 above D-min (Status M, CN-16). The blue-sensitive curve is wavy (small bumps near 430 and 450 nm) before its peak at about 470 nm.",
   "sensitivity: each layer ends in a steep drop (blue ends about 495 nm, green starts about 462 nm and ends about 600 nm, red starts about 558 nm and ends about 700 nm); the tool keeps one y per x, so the last few nm of the drops are approximated, and the first/last sample on the 5 nm grid can be a flat extension of up to 5 nm.",
   "dyes: the plot is not of separate cyan/magenta/yellow dyes but of two curves, Mid-scale Density (mid; typical densities for a mid-scale neutral subject) and Minimum Density (min; D-min), 400-720 nm, y = spectral diffuse density as printed (0-2.5)."
  ]
